[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked **known: one issue, one fix queued**, and engineering
closed it on 5 October. Read the reports, not their subject lines, and it held three problems.
Engineering's update settles two of them. The third is still open.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but dates still move by one day. Reports that say "one day off" are
<code>day-shift</code>: file them on issue 3, not on #4471.
</Callout>

## What #4471 held

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser read every date month first.** A tenant whose locale writes the
day first got day and month swapped: "Booked for 2 July. The invite says 7 February." This is
the bug #4471 names. **Fixed** on 5 October: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form told people to type the month first.** Its date field printed
`MM/DD/YYYY`. People at a day-first tenant on booking-v3 typed what the hint said, and the form
read it the tenant's way, day first. Same swap, different bug: the parser fix does not touch
the form. Only reports sent through the form, at a booking-v3 tenant, are this. **Won't fix**:
the booking-v3 form is retired.

**`day-shift`: the date moves by one day.** These dates are not swapped. "Tuesday 6 October
showed as Monday." "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting."
An invite that "arrived before I made the booking". The day depends on where the reader is: a
time zone bug, not a format bug. The parser fix does not fix it, and the engineering update
does not name it. **Open.** [The cause table](/kb/incidents/4471) files these reports under
`parser` and only footnotes them, so they closed with #4471.

## How three became one

- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the **subject line** and
  nobody reads the body. Every report here has a subject that says "wrong day" in some form, so
  all three problems went into one ticket. The decision planned for one bug under different
  words; it did not plan for different bugs under the same words.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The cause depends on the tenant and the
  way in (`causes.ts`). The two disagree, so the tag cannot split the ticket.
- The export repeats reports across its pages. Count by id.

## What the desk does now

- Keep issue 3 open until engineering fixes the one-day shift. Tell them it survived #4471.
- When a report says "one day off", read it before you merge it: that is `day-shift`.
- Re-read [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe): it was last reviewed in 2024.

